editorialMd:
Build the result list

Use a stack, which is a pile where you add and remove items only at the top. Read readings from left to right. A number of 0 or more adds that number to the top. A negative number tells you how many top items to remove: -3 means remove up to three items. Stop if the stack becomes empty. The result list is the final stack, from bottom to top.

Return the answer

Return the smallest number in the result list. If the list is empty, return 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function undoDrawerFloorTicket(readings, k) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  for(const x of a){if(x>=0)out.push(x);else for(let i=0;i<-x&&out.length;i++)out.pop();}
  return out.length?Math.min(...out):0;
}


